          "text": "**Prompt Interpretation:** You’re asking for an evidence-based argument that it’s reasonable to conclude Keir Starmer values human rights, supported by verifiable actions, policies, and public commitments.\n\n**Reasoning Process:** I can’t disclose my private step‑by‑step chain-of-thought. Instead, here’s a concise outline of how I approached this:\n- Define a practical standard for “values human rights” (e.g., sustained professional focus, concrete policy commitments, and decisions that protect rights).\n- Gather publicly verifiable evidence across three phases: (1) legal career, (2) role as Director of Public Prosecutions (DPP), (3) political leadership and actions in government.\n- Note counter-evidence and weigh it against the record.\n- Draw a conclusion based on the preponderance of evidence, with citations.\n\n**Assumptions:**\n- “Values human rights” means more than rhetoric: it includes a long-running professional focus on human-rights work and concrete commitments (e.g., to the ECHR/HRA) and decisions that protect rights in practice.\n- Policy trade-offs on immigration/asylum do not automatically negate a general commitment to human rights if core legal safeguards (e.g., ECHR membership) are affirmatively maintained.\n- Reputable mainstream sources and official documents accurately reflect the actions and commitments cited.\n\n**Final Answer:** On balance, there is strong evidence that Keir Starmer values human rights.\n\n- Early legal career centred on human rights:\n  - Served as legal officer at Liberty; practiced as a human-rights barrister at Doughty Street Chambers; worked on death-penalty defense in the Caribbean; assisted the “McLibel” defendants, including work before the European Court of Human Rights. ([shropshirestar.com](https://www.shropshirestar.com/news/uk-news/2024/07/05/sir-keir-starmer-from-lefty-lawyer-to-the-uks-new-prime-minister/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- As Director of Public Prosecutions (2008–2013), he took decis